## 1. Introduction
The increasing demand for secondary batteries in the field of fabrication of reusable new energy storage has resulted in the development of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) as they are environmentally friendly and characterized by high energy density and long cycle life [1] Silicon (Si) is a highly desirable anode material as it is characterized by excellent theoretical capacity [2] The charging potential of the Si anode is higher than that of graphite (0.4 V vs. $\mathrm{{Li/Li^{+}}}$ . The use of this material helps avoid the growth of lithium dendrites on the Si anode [3 4 . Significantly high changes in the volume of the Si anode, resulting in the generation of high amounts of stress, accelerate the process of fracture of the electrode structure, resulting in the separation of the active material from the collector. The disrupted internal conductive network of the electrode causes a rapid decrease in the cell capacity, resulting in large volume variations in the Si anode. This eventually results in the generation of high amounts of stress [5] In addition, the low electronic conductivity $(10^{-5}–10^{-3}\textrm{S c m}^{-1})$ of Si results in slow electrochemical reaction kinetics, which also limits the practical application of the material [6]
Currently, various strategies exist to suppress the volume effect of Si, improve the electrical conductivity of Si-based materials, and construct stable solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) films on Si anode [7] In general, the particle size of Si can be decreased to effectively shorten the transmission path of lithium-ion, alleviate the internal stress of the particles, ensure the structural integrity of the material, and improve the cycle stability of Si anode [8 9 . However, the nanosized Si particles tend to present a high specific surface area and form unstable SEI layers upon lithiation/delithiation process, resulting in an increase in the extent of consumption of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and the generation of low initial coulombic efficiency (ICE). Additionally, complex synthesis processes and high costs limit the commercialization of nanosized Si [10] Various processes, such as the surface coating of Si and the formation of egg yolk–shell and porous structures, were explored to enhance the lithium storage performance of Si anodes and widen the application prospects of the materials [11] Nitrogen-doped porous siliconcarbon composites have been proven to be one of the most effective materials [12] Nitrogen-doped porous carbon exhibits high electronic/ionic conductivity and its porous structure provides additional buffer space for structural variation, which improves the performance of LIBs [13] For instance, Si particles with double cladding layers $({\mathrm{Si}}@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{x}@C-{\mathrm{NB}})$ exhibit excellent electrochemical performance [14] Additionally, a self-supporting and binder-free N, O-co-doped 3D vertical graphene carbon nanofiber electrode (VGAs@Si@CNFs) with ultra-high Si content delivered high specific capacity $(3619.5\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.05\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}.$ and outstanding rate performance $\mathsf{'}1093.1\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 1500 cycles at $8{\textrm{A g}}^{-1}$ [15] Recently, self-supporting 3D structures of nitrogen-doped graphene composites containing Si and a layer of silicate $\left(\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{G}\ @\mathsf{S i-}30@\mathsf{H S i}\right)$ on the outer layer were developed as Si anode materials. These materials could be operated at 5 and $10~\mathsf{C}$ over 10,000 cycles. The capacities recorded were 817 and $537\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ respectively [16] It could be inferred that silicon-based anodes composed of N-doped porous carbon could effectively improve the electrochemical performance of LIBs.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived porous carbon materials, have been extensively applied as a frame for encapsulating other materials to improve their performances [17–19] The insitu binding of Si particles to MOFs can help improve the conductivity of the Si anode [20] The as-generated carbon structure containing numerous holes and vacancies formed by the removal of organic ligands accelerates the process of insertion and extraction of ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ This also, hinders the "emergence of dead lithium" [21] Heat treatment further improves the structural and chemical stability of MOFs. The stability of MOFs as electrode materials is maintained during the charging and discharging processes [22] Numerous researchers have reported that MOF-derived carbon systems can hinder the process of volume expansion and improve the electronic conductivity of Si-based materials [20] Mo/W-based hybridized zeolite imidazole frameworks (HZIFs), as a branch of MOFs, contain a great quantity of zeolite-like inorganic oxygenated acid units $(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}/\mathrm{WO}_{4})$ in their structures [23 24 . The presence of the Mo/W component imparts excellent mechanical stability to hybridized ZIF, which can be formed into well-ordered pores following chemical and thermal transformation [25] Currently, most of the reported ZIF materials or their derivatives are usually formed using the first row of transition metals (Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Zn, etc.). The formation of bimetallic hybridized ZIF systems is likely to enhance the lithium storage capacity of the materials, and this field has rarely been explored [26 27 .
Here, we synthesized $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ by introducing Si particles in situ into N-doped carbon polyhedral micro cage derived from Co-Mo bimetallic MOF (hybridized ZIF-Co/Mo) via a MOF selftemplated synthesis strategy. Compared to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ prepared from monometallic MOF (ZIF-67) doped Si, Si/CoMo@NCP exhibits better capacity and stability. Also, the structural properties of the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ composites, which contain multiple components, buffer the volume change in Si during the lithiation/delithiation process. More than that, the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ exhibited a high reversible capacity of $745\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 400 cycles, and the capacity retention rate was close to $98\%$ Kinetic analysis reveals that $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ operates following a mixed lithium storage mechanism that involves the alloying of Si, as well as multiple electron transfer reactions associated with the metallic cobalt and its oxides (Co, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ during the lithiation/delithiation process, which helps improve the lithium storage performance of the material.
## 2. Results and discussion
The synthetic strategy associated with the formation of Si/CoMo@NCP is schematically displayed in Fig. 1(a) Detailedly, the Co atom from cobalt nitrate coordinates with the N atom of the dimethylimidazole and the O atom of the $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}-$ unit to form
HZIF-CoMo through the solvothermal reaction process. The HZIF material possesses lots of ordered pores available as a carrier to encapsulate Si nanoparticles to construct the Si/ZIF-CoMo. Subsequently forming porous N-doped carbon polyhedra $({\mathrm{Si}}/{\mathrm{CoMo@NCP}})$ following the process of pyrolysis. Figs. S(1) and S(2) (Supporting Information) present the structural and morphological information of the precursor, which proves the successful preparation of Si/ZIF-CoMo and Si/ZIF-67. Analysis of SEM images (Fig. 1 b–e)) reveals that ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ exhibit uniform polyhedral structures inherited from their precursors, and the size of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ particle ${\cdot}{\sim}6~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , Fig. 1(c)  was larger than that of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ ${\cdot}{\sim}2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , Fig. 1(e) . The removal of organics from ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ during the carbonization process resulted in the surface collapse of the material (Fig. 1(b) and (c ) [23] In contrast, ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ was robust, with curved tubes distributed uniformly on the surface and a few aggregations of large particles. TEM images revealed the polyhedral structure of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ which could be identified from the outlines of the thin edge and thick middle area of the material (Fig. 1(f) . The lattice fringes with the spacing of $0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $0.24\ \mathrm{nm}$ correspond to (111) and (311) crystal planes of Co and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ respectively (Fig. $1(\mathrm{g})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ . The crystal plane spacing of $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ was attributed to $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ (-111), and the curved lattice stripes corresponded to the graphitized carbon nanotubes (CNTs), which were derived under conditions of catalytic Co [28] Analysis of the element distribution profiles (Fig. $1(\mathrm{h}{-}0))$ depicts that uniformly distributed Si is fully encapsulated by a carbon shell derived from MOFs, and it forms a composite with other elements. Moreover, the uniform distribution of C, N, O, Co, and Mo elements was observed in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}.$
The crystallinities of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ (Fig. 2(a) and (b ) were analyzed by comparing their XRD patterns. The sharp diffraction peaks corresponding to the composites appeared at approximately 44 , which was indexed as metallic Co (PDF#15- 0806). Meanwhile, the diffraction peaks appearing at $28.4^{\circ}$ were assigned to the (111) plane of the cubic phase of Si (PDF#27-1402). The patterns recorded for $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ presented several other diffraction peaks at $18^{\circ}$ , $26^{\circ}$ , and $31^{\circ}$ and these were attributed to the (-101), (-111), and (101) crystal planes of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ (PDF#32-0671), respectively. The peaks at $19^{\circ}$ , $36^{\circ}$ , and $44^{\circ}$ , were the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (PDF#42-1467), reflecting the (111), (311), and (400) crystal planes, respectively [29] Fig. 2(c) presents the Raman spectral profiles recorded for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}.$ The peaks at 495 and $923~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to Si, and those at 286 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ correspond to Co [28] It is noteworthy that $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ presents prominent $\mathsf{D}/\mathsf{G}$ peaks. The $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio was calculated to be 1.17, and the peaks appeared near $1335~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (D peak) and 1567 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (G peak). The $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ value corresponding to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ was recorded to be 0.95. The order of graphitization of the samples was $\mathrm{Si}/{C0@\mathrm{NCP}}>\mathrm{Si}/{C0\mathrm{Mo@NCP}},$ and the order could be attributed to the fact that a large amount of carbon was present in the latter precursor. This reduced the catalytic effect of Co on C. Additionally, the low degree of graphitization of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ indicated the presence of defects in the material, and this promoted $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ storage [30 31 .
To measure the specific surface area and pore size distribution of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms and pore size distribution curves were obtained and given in Fig. 2(d) and (e , respectively. The specific surface areas corresponding to $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ were 309.2 and $72.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, and the areas were determined following the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method. Typical type-IV isotherms were recorded for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ and a hysteresis loop was recorded in the $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}$ range of 0.4–1. The Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) analysis method was used to arrive at the results, and the pore size distribution was approximately $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ [32 33 . The origin of these mesopores could be potentially attributed to the collapse of the ZIF-67 framework during the carbonization process and the volatilization of organic matter. The generation of mesopores after post pyrolysis is likely due to the collapse of the ZIF-67 framework and the volatilization of organic matter [34] Compared with $\mathsf{S i}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},\mathsf{S i}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ shows typical type-I isotherm characteristics, and more diversified mesoporous size distributions of $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $23\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and $34~\mathrm{nm}$ . These results indicated that the presence of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ particles in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ resulted in a significant change in the structure of the N-doped carbon matrix. Furthermore, $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ with porosity was more conducive to increasing the contact area between the electrolyte and the active material and helped improve the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ storage capacity [35]
Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ were applied, which are displayed in Fig. 2(f) The TG curve of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ reveals a small decrease in mass in the temperature range of $30{\mathrm{-}}200\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}.$ . The decrease in mass in this region could be attributed to the volatilization of residual water. Owing to the oxidation of $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ and Co, an increase in mass at $277^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The TG curve of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ shows a similar increase at $240~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , due also to the oxidation of Co. The mass of both samples continued to decrease, and this could be attributed to the oxidation of the carbon matrix to gaseous ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ under conditions of increasing temperature [36] TGA analysis revealed that the carbon contents in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ were $17.98\%$ and $35.79\%$ , respectively. When the temperature exceeded $600~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the increase in mass of both materials results from the oxidation of Si to $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ The difference between the TG curves of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o@N C P i s}$ originated from the oxidation of different nanoparticles in the air atmosphere during heat treatment [37]
The chemical composition and valence states of the elements on the near-surface of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ were investigated using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Fig. S3(a) and (b) (Supporting Information), the atomic concentrations of C, N, Mo, Si, and Co in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ were $51.9\%$ $32\%$ , $9.3\%$ , $3.0\%$ , and $3.7\%$ , respectively. In contrast, the atomic concentrations of C, N, Si, and Co were $79.1\%$ , $6.6\%$ , $12.1\%$ , and $2.3\%$ , respectively, in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ As illustrated in Fig. $2(\mathrm{g)}$ the C 1s spectral profiles corresponding to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ could be deconvoluted into four peaks. The peaks at $284.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{=C}}$ groups of the two materials. The three peaks of low intensity appeared at 285.4, 285.8, and 289.1 eV of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ and the peaks at 285.5, 285.9, and $288.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ that were assigned to the C-N, C-O and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ groups, respectively [38] The high-resolution N1s spectrum (Fig. S4(a), Supporting Information) shows similar three peaks at 398.5, 399.7, and 401.1 eV for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ and 398.6, 399.2, 400.8 eV for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ that are ascribed to the pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N, respectively. More importantly, Mo-N bond formation was observed at $397.2\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ (Fig. S4(b), Supporting Information), and the peak at 395.7 eV was due to the presence of Mo $3{\tt p}3/2$ [39] This reflects that $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ could bond be tightly to N, which helps to improve the stability of the material [40]
From the high-resolution XPS spectrum of Si (Fig. 2(i) , the Si $2{\tt p}$ peaks at 103.9, 102.7, 101.7, and $100.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}.$ Peaks appeared at 104.1, 103.6, 101.8, and $100.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the profiles recorded for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}.$ These peaks were attributed to the $\mathsf{S i}^{4+}$ $\mathsf{S i}^{3+}$ $\mathsf{S i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{S i^{+}}$ respectively [41] The lower binding energy peak at $99.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is unoxidized Si atoms. The $\mathsf{S i}^{4+}$ $\mathsf{S i}^{3+}$ $\mathrm{Si}^{2+}$ $\mathsf{S i^{+}}$ $S\mathrm{i}^{0}$ ratio was found to be $27.8\%$ : $38.5\%$ : $17.1\%$ : $12.9\%$ : $3.7\%$ , and the results were deduced from the peak intensities of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}.$ In the case of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ the corresponding ratio was $28.4\%$ $18.5\%$ : $11.0\%$ : $31.6\%$ : $10.5\%$ based on the peak intensities. The average valence state of Si was estimated to be $\sim2.7$ for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ and this corresponded to the value of $\scriptstyle x=1.3$ in $\mathrm{SiO}_{x}$ This value was lower than the valence of $\mathrm{SiO}_{1.8}$ in the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}.$ The relatively lower value of Si in $\mathrm{SiO}_{x}$ should afford higher reactivity [42] In the Co 2p XPS spectra (Fig. $2(\mathrm{h})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ , the peaks at 796.7 and 780.1 eV were attributed to the $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of $C0^{3+}$ The $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peaks of $C0^{2+}$ appear at $\sim798.6$ $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and 783.2 eV $(2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the peaks at 785.6 and 802.4 eV are two satellite peaks [43] For ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ the Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ XPS peaks at 232.8 and $235.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S4(b), Supporting Information) reveal the oxidized $\mathrm{Mo^{6+}}$ valence state on the surface of the sample. In addition, the peaks of $\mathsf{M o}^{4+}~(3\mathsf{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ centered at 230.8 and 234.1 eV can also be recognized [44]
The electrochemical performances of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ samples as anodes for LIBs were evaluated by CV testing. Fig. 3(a) displays the CV curves of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ in the potential range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scan rate of $0.1\ \mathrm{\mV}\ s^{-1}$ The reduction peak at approximately $1.35~\mathrm{V}$ corresponds to the process of $C0^{3+}$ being reduced to $C0^{2+}$ while the inflection point at 0.67 V corresponds to the reduction process of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{0}}^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ Eqs. (1) and ( 2) , and similar results were obtained for the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ (Fig. S5, Supporting Information) [45] Differently, the peak at $0.27\mathrm{~V~}$ was attributed to the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{Co}$ on the surface of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ anode Eq. (3) . The peaks at 0.57 and $1.12\mathrm{~V~}$ of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ respectively, indicate the formation of an SEI film [46 47 . Additionally, the peak below $0.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ corresponds to the dealloying of $\operatorname{Li}_{X}\operatorname{Si}$ (Eq. (4)  [48] Also, two pairs of redox peaks $(1.72/1.54\$ and $1.42/1.25\mathrm{~V)}$ can be found, and these were attributed to the phase transitions of $\mathsf{L i}_{x}\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r}{0<x<1.}\end{array}$ from monoclinic to orthorhombic and vice versa (Eqs. (5) and ( 6)  [49] Besides, the oxidation peak at $1.26\mathrm{~V~}$ was assigned to the oxidation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ to $C0^{2+}$ in $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ and the wide peak at $2.14\mathrm{~V~}$ related to the conversion of ${\mathsf{C}}0^{2+}$ to $C0^{3+}$ and the process of lithium-ion stripping of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}.$ In addition, the anodic peaks at $0.45\mathrm{~V~}$ for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ and 0.31 and $0.52\mathrm{~V~}$ for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ are raised from the de-alloying process of the $\operatorname{Li}_{x}\operatorname{Si}$ phase [50] Meanwhile, the above analyses illustrate that in addition to the alloying/de-alloying reactions of Si in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ Co, Mo and their oxides such as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ were also associated with the lithium storage process through multiple electron transfer reactions. Undoubtedly, multiple lithiation reaction mechanisms can effectively improve the storage of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ The overall electrochemical reaction equations of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ anode are as follows:
$$
\mathrm{Co3O_{4}}+2\mathrm{Li^{+}}+2\mathrm{e^{-}}\rightleftharpoons3\mathrm{CoO}+\mathrm{Li_{2}O}
$$
$$
\mathsf{C o O}\ +\ 2\mathrm{Li^{+}}+2\mathsf{e^{-}}\ \rightleftharpoons\mathsf{C o}\ +\ \mathrm{Li_{2}O}
$$
$$
x\mathrm{Li}^{+}+\mathsf{C o}+x\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathsf{C o}
$$
$$
{\mathsf{S i}}+x{\mathsf{L i^{+}}}+x{\mathsf{e^{-}}}\rightleftharpoons{\mathsf{L i}}_{x}{\mathsf{S i}}
$$
$$
3\mathrm{Li}^{+}+3\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{LiMoO}_{2}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{M}\mathrm{}\mathrm{}0+2\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{}0
$$
The galvanostatic charging discharging profiles (GCDs) with the different current densities for the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ electrode are shown in Fig. 3(b) Significantly, the discharge-specific capacity was up to $1615\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with $65\%$ of the ICE in the first cycle. The relatively large irreversible capacity is associated with the formation of the SEI film, irreversible lithiation products [51–52] Upon increasing the current density to $2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ the reversible capacities of the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ electrode showed a slow decrease and retained at 723 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ Fig. 3(c) compares the rate performances of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anodes. When the current densities were 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, and 2.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ the reversible specific capacities of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ are 1162, 1108, 1057, 932, and $723\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ respectively. As the current density is recovered from 2.0 to 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ the reversible specific capacity of the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ can be recovered to 1299 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ By contrast, ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrode shows lower lithiation capacities of 1099, 912, 755, 612, and $454~\mathrm{mAh}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, recovering only to $980\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ Pronounced, Si/CoMo@NCP electrode exhibits higher lithium storage while the charge/discharge with a high current.
To characterize the cycling stability of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anodes, the prolonged galvanostatic charge-discharge tests were investigated. At $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ anode exhibits an excellent electrochemical capacity of 1013 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles (Fig. 3(d) . In contrast, under the same test conditions, the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anode can only maintain $857\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ At a higher current density of $1.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ the cycling performance between $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ is more different (Fig. 3(e) . The ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ anode shows preferable capacity retention (specific discharge capacity of $745~\mathrm{\mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 400 cycles), well above that of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ (478 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Compared to previously reported MOF-derived $\mathsf{S i@C}$ composites (Fig. S6 and Table S1, Supporting Information), the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ exhibits superior lithium storage activity. The improvement in the lithium storage ability of the systems could be attributed to various factors: (1) In addition to Si, both $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ are also involved in the lithiation process, allowing more $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ to participate in the electrochemical reaction, resulting in an increase in capacity retention ability. (2) Multiple types of doped N enhanced ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ and electron transport. (3) Diversity of electrode active compositions not only helped improved electrical conductivity but also buffered the volume change during lithiation, thus reinforces the cycling stability.
To explore the basic lithium storage mechanism of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ the composition evolution of the electrode for the first cycle was studied by ex-situ XRD (Fig. 3(f) . Overall, the lithium storage of the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ primarily follows the alloying reaction between Si and Li, as well as the multi-electron transfer reaction between transition metal oxides and lithium. From the initial state to discharge to $0.13~\mathrm{V},$ , the peaks of Si at $21^{\circ}$ and $28^{\circ}$ are gradually disappearing, corresponding to the transformation process of $\mathrm{Li}_{12}\mathrm{Si}_{7}$ $(21.4^{\circ},23.8^{\circ})$ . As the cycling process progressed, when charged to $0.46~\mathsf{V},$ amorphization of Si occurs irreversibly in the discharge cycle, resulting in a disappearance of Si peaks in subsequent cycles [53] However, the appearance of the peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0$ could be attributed to the side reactions occurring during the lithiation process [4] Correspondingly, the multistep reduction of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to CoO and Co occurred when the system was discharged to 0.81 V. The peak of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ reappeared when charged to $2.17\mathrm{V}.$ When discharged to $1.5\mathrm{V}$ , $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ was lithiated forming $\mathrm{Li}_{0.98}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ and the intensity of the peak corresponding to this compound gradually weakened until it was charged to $1.7~\mathrm{V}.$ . At this point, the $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ peak re-appeared. Overall, the characteristic peaks of Si, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ were found in both initial and final states. This reproducibility indicated that the reaction has high reversibility and strong cycling stability [54].
Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S8(a) plot the variation of differential capacitance $({\mathrm{d}}Q_{\mathrm{m}}/{\mathrm{d}}V)$ curves of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anodes. During the first five cycles at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and then continued at $0.3\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ for five cycles. In the process of discharge, a typical $\mathrm{Si}\xrightarrow{}\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathrm{Si}$ peak appeared at about $0.1\mathrm{~V~}$ , and a reduction peak of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}{\to}{\mathsf{C o}}$ appeared at $0.7~\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ (Fig. S7(a), Supporting Information). At the same time, the lithiation peaks of Mo formed $\operatorname{Li}_{x}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}0_{2}$ are located at $1.1-1.5\mathrm{~V}.$ . In addition, the peaks of $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ during the charging process belong to $\mathrm{Li}_{X}\mathrm{Si}\xrightarrow{}\mathrm{Si}$ , and the peaks of $1.2\mathrm{V}$ are corresponding to the oxidation of ${\cal C}0{\rightarrow}{\cal C}0^{2+}$ (Fig. S7(a) and (b), Supporting Information). Furthermore, for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ the delithiation peaks at $1.8~\mathrm{V}$ and $2.1~\mathrm{V}$ are attributed to the process of lithium-ion stripping from $\mathsf{M o O}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}{\to}{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ respectively.
To explore further and compare the diffusion kinetics of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ at ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrodes, adopting the Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) test. The as-obtained ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ diffusion coefficient $(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+})$ values calculated from the GITT curves at the lithiation/delithiation states of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrodes are shown in Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S8(b) (Supporting Information). $\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}$ is calculated based on Eq. (7) as follows [55]
$$
D_{\mathrm{{Li^{+}}}}=\frac{4L^{2}}{\pi\tau}\bigg(\frac{\Delta E_{\mathrm{{S}}}}{\Delta E_{\mathrm{{t}}}}\bigg)^{2}
$$
where $L$ is the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ diffusion length for a compact electrode, i.e. the electrode thickness; $\tau$ (s) represents the relaxation time; $\Delta E_{\mathsf{S}}$ (V) is the steady-state potential change by the current pulse. $\Delta E_{\mathrm{t}}$ (V) is the potential change during the constant current pulse after eliminating the iR drop. The $D_{\mathrm{Li}}\mathrm{^+}$ in the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ ranges from $1.7\times10^{-11}$ to $2.3\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ during the discharge process and $3.0\times10^{-11}$ to $4.4\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ during the charging process, which are higher than that in S $\operatorname{i}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ (ranges from $3.5\times10^{-11}$ to $1.6\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ s^{-1}$ of the discharge process and from $6.3\times10^{-11}$ to $3.8\times10^{-11}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ during the charging process). The higher $\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Li+}}$ for $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ is probably caused by its conductive components, higher specific surface area, as well as abundant pore structure as aforementioned.
To further investigate the lithium storage kinetics of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrodes, the CV curves at scan rates of 0.2 to $1.0\ \mathrm{\mV}s^{-1}$ were measured. In Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S8(c) (Supporting Information), the shapes of CV curves for both electrodes are similar. The intensities of redox peaks gradually increased as the scanning speed increased due to the electrochemical polarization [56] As seen in Fig. 4(d) and Fig. S8(d) (Supporting Information), the value of b indicates the diffusion type for ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ which can be obtained from the slope of the $\log(V).$ - $\log(i)$ plot. The b values are 0.78 and 0.98 for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ electrode and 0.46 and 0.57 for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrode, respectively. The capacitance ratio increases with the scanning speed, and the proportions of capacitive contribution were $86\%$ , $88\%$ $90\%$ $93\%$ , and $98\%$ , respectively for $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ anode (Fig. 4(e) and (  ). In contrast, the proportions of capacitive contribution for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anode were $47\%$ , $56\%$ , $66\%$ $74\%$ , and $81\%$ , respectively (Fig. S8(e) and (f)), Supporting Information). We, believe that the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ is dominated by the surface-controlled capacitive contribution, which improves rate capacity and cycling stability.
Ex-situ electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (ex-situ EIS) was used to evaluate the stability of SEI film on the surface, thus responding to the electrochemical kinetics of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrodes at different potentials. A comparison of the Nyquist plots generated for the electrodes is presented in Figs. $4(\mathbf{g})$ and $S8(\mathbf{g})$ (Supporting Information). The corresponding fitting curves were obtained by analyzing the equivalent circuits. In addition, the variation of the peak position of the electrodes was observed through a contour map (Figs. 4(h) and S8(h), Supporting Information). In the Nyquist plots, the high-frequency semicircle reflects the $R_{\mathrm{SEI}}$ while the mid-frequency semicircle indicates the Rct. Also, the straight line indicates the diffusion of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ in the active electrode material as defined by Warburg impedance [57] At the beginning of the discharge process, both $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ anodes showed high $R_{\mathrm{SEI}}$ values and no significant fluctuations. Probably, the surface of the particles is covered by a complete carbon layer, which avoids direct contact between the particles and the electrolyte during the initial state Thus, a thin SEI is initially formed [58] In addition, the $R_{\mathrm{SEI}}$ values of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ remained at 28 and $51~\Omega$ respectively before the first discharge to $1.5~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. In the subsequent discharge process, as the voltage further decreases, the $R_{\mathrm{SEI}}$ fluctuation of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ is lower than that of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ indicating that there is a thin and stable SEI on the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ surface. This layer could help improve the specific capacity retention ability and cycling stability of the materials [59] However, for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}},$ the kinetics of embedded lithium/delithiation is decreased following the constant formation/decomposition of SEI on the particle surface. The Rct value corresponding to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ decreased gradually $(\sim24\Omega)$ when charged to $1.0\ \mathrm{V},$ , and the Rct value corresponding to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ $(\sim34~\Omega)$ gradually decreased when charged to $0.25~\mathrm{V}.$ According to the relationship between $Z^{\cdot}$ and $\omega^{-1/2}$ for ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ in the low-frequency region of EIS (Fig. S9(a) and (b), Supporting Information)), it was calculated that the ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ transfer coefficient of ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ recorded at different potentials was 3-9 times higher than the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}.$ Overall, the impedance of $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ increases less than ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ with the increase of potential. This illustrated that the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ has better electrochemical reaction kinetics as a result of the porous structure and high specific surface area [58]
To evaluate the utility value of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}},$ the full cells were constructed with $\mathsf{N C M}_{811}$ cathode. Fig. S10(a) (Supporting Information) displays the operating schematic of the full cell $\mathrm{(NCM_{811}//S i/C o M o@N C P)}$ , also the charge-discharge platform for the first five cycles is depicted in Fig. S10(b) (Supporting Information), with an electrochemical voltage window of $2.3\substack{-4.0\mathrm{~V~}}$ at 0.5 C ( $1\mathrm{~C~}=175\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ . The $\mathrm{NCM}_{811}//\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{CoMo@NCP}$ provides initial charge/discharge capacities of ca. 107 and 174 mAh ${\tt g}^{-1}$ (ICE $\approx61\%$ ). Meanwhile, the specific capacity can still achieve 71 mAh ${\bf g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles (Fig. S10(c), Supporting Information), confirming that $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ is well applied for full cell and has well practicality.
The variations in the volumes of the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ and ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrodes after charge/discharge cycling were examined by SEM. Fig. 5 presents the cross-sectional view, and it can be found that the thickness of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ electrode varies from 21 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 5(a)  to $23.6~\mu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 5(e) , resulting in a volume expansion of $12.4\%$ As a comparison, the thickness of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ electrode changes from $26.7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 5(c)  to $34~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. $5(\mathbf{g}),$ , and a significantly high extent of volume expansion $(27.3\%)$ . Analysis of the top view, slight surface cracks can be observed on $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ electrode (Fig. 5 b and f)) after cycling, but there were deep cracks occurred in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ (Fig. 5 d and h)), and this could be attributed to uneven stress distribution caused by the presence of nanoparticles as active substances. By comparing the changes in the polyhedron structure of the electrodes before and after the cycle (Fig. 5 i–p)). It was observed that the MOF-derived carbon system retained its polyhedron structure during the lithiation/delithiation cycling. Specifically, after cycling, the polyhedron structure in ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ electrode has no obvious rupture or deformation and maintains the inherent porous structure, with thin SEI on the surface (Fig. $5(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{n}))$ . However, the polyhedral structure of the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}$ accumulated and deformed, forming a thick and dense SEI film on the surface (Fig. $5(\mathsf{o},\mathsf{p}),$ . Consequently, it can be pointed out that the introduction of $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}$ into $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ particles is helpful to construct the carbon skeleton of the Mo-N bond, and the presence of a variety of metal oxide also inhibits the agglomeration and expansion of Si in the cycle. This also ensures the structural integrity and has excellent cycle performance.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we successfully synthesized carbon polyhedralencapsulated Si anode derived from Co-Mo bimetal MOFs utilizing the self-template synthesis method combined with the pyrolysis strategy. The as-synthesized ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ reflects excellent lithium storage properties, including large capacity and high rate capability. Specifically, the ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ exhibits a high reversible specific capacity of 1013 mAh ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at 0.5 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, and excellent long-cycle stability with a reversible specific capacity of $745\:\mathrm{\mAh\:\mathrm{\g}^{-1}}$ for 400 cycles at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ Kinetic studies show that the $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}$ have higher capacitive charge contributions and larger ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ diffusion coefficients, as well as lower resistances, compared to ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o@N C P}}.$ In addition, it is also demonstrated that ${\mathsf{S i}}/{\mathsf{C o M o@N C P}}$ has a hybridized lithium storage mechanism with multiple electron transfer reactions and alloying reactions. More importantly, the as-constructed $\mathrm{NCM}_{811}//\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{CoMo@NCP}$ full cells also deliver good energy storage performance and potential for practical applications. Overall, the work demonstrates that bimetallic MOFs can be well used for the construction of carbon polyhedral-encapsulated Si anode materials for Li-ion battery storage systems.
